Lambda expressions introduced a clear and concise way to represent anonymous functions in Java. They drastically reduce boilerplate code by **eliminating the need to write bulky anonymous inner classes**. 

A lambda expression essentially provides the implementation for the single abstract method of a Functional Interface on the fly. Its syntax is `(parameters) -> expression` or `(parameters) -> { statements; }`.

**Before Java 8:**
<pre><code class="language-java">Runnable r = new Runnable() {
    @Override
    public void run() { System.out.println("Running..."); }
};</code></pre>

**After Java 8 (Lambda):**
<pre><code class="language-java">Runnable r = () -> System.out.println("Running...");</code></pre>

This paradigm shift enabled Java to embrace functional programming, making advanced APIs like Streams possible.

